-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c715a5ec-fabf-5e3c-b191-c6859aba3332', 'rg9-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Many Names for Used$q$, $q$Walk through any secondhand shop and you will notice that almost nothing is described as "used." Sweaters are "gently worn." Bicycles are "previously owned." A lamp with a chipped base might carry a card that calls it "full of character." Each of these phrases refers to the same plain fact: someone else had the item first.

Why do sellers avoid the simplest word? The answer has to do with connotation, the feelings and associations a word carries in addition to its dictionary meaning. "Used" is accurate, but it can suggest something tired, dirty, or close to the end of its life. "Previously owned" sounds neutral and businesslike. "Gently worn" goes further, inviting the shopper to imagine a careful former owner who treated the sweater well.

Some phrases are clear euphemisms, softer expressions chosen to make a fact easier to accept. When a jacket is labeled "ready for a new adventure," the seller is not claiming the jacket has plans. The phrase simply asks the buyer to think about the future instead of the past.

Other words push in the opposite direction. People sometimes call donated goods "castoffs" or "hand-me-downs." The first suggests that something was thrown away because it had no value. The second is warmer, connected to families and siblings, even though it describes the same kind of object.

None of these words is a lie. A gently worn sweater really was owned before, and a used bicycle really may be full of character. Still, shoppers who notice the choice of words can separate the facts about an item from the feelings the label is trying to create. That skill is useful far beyond the thrift store.$q$, 'draft')
