   $q$Mr. Abernathy says the car is "just catching its breath," and Aunt Rosalind says, "I see you came the long way." What do these two remarks have in common?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both describe a problem in gentle words that spare the family's feelings.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Both adults are teasing the family harshly for their bad planning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Both adults are giving exact directions to the lake house.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"},{"text":"Both remarks show that the adults believe the car is ruined for good.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both describe a problem in gentle words that spare the family's feelings.$q$, $q$Each remark softens an unpleasant fact (an overheated engine, a trip three hours late) into something mild, and Mom "finally laughed" in response. The passage says Mr. Abernathy "did not lecture or tease," so the remarks are not harsh.$q$, 'draft', 'b529d6f7-7092-5e3d-9d11-a9c17f8c195f')
