-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('722083c6-6b19-5207-9ec3-f9fcc6af7d55', 'rg9-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Ocean's Quick-Change Artist$q$, $q$Few animals are as good at disappearing as the octopus. One moment it is a bright orange shape crawling across the sea floor. The next, it has vanished, leaving behind what looks like an ordinary patch of rock and algae.

The octopus manages this trick with its skin. Just beneath the surface are thousands of tiny sacs of color called chromatophores. Each sac is surrounded by small muscles. When the muscles pull, the sac stretches open and the color spreads; when they relax, the color shrinks to a dot. Because the octopus can control these muscles quickly, its skin works like a living screen, changing patterns in less than a second.

Color is only part of the disguise. Many octopuses can also raise small bumps on their skin, turning smooth flesh into something that looks rough like coral or wrinkled like seaweed. Scientists sometimes describe this as hiding in plain sight, since the animal is right there in the open but does not register as an animal at all.

Researchers have also noticed that octopuses seem curious. In aquariums, they investigate new objects, unscrew lids, and squeeze through openings barely wider than their eyes. Some keepers describe them as mischievous, a word that suggests playful trouble rather than real harm. Others prefer "inquisitive," which sounds more scientific.

The octopus has no shell, no armor, and very few defenses besides its speed and its ink. In a sea full of hungry predators, its soft, boneless body might seem like a weakness. Its skin turns that weakness into one of the most impressive survival tools in the ocean.$q$, 'draft')
