-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d9955455-305e-502d-934b-7b6201903b64', 'rg9-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Aisle Nine$q$, $q$My first job was stocking shelves at a grocery store on Fairmont Avenue. The manager, Ms. Petrakis, gave me a name tag, a box cutter with a safety blade, and one piece of advice: "Never say 'I don't know' to a customer. Say 'Let me find out.'"

On my third Saturday, a man in a gray jacket stopped me in aisle nine. He wanted a particular kind of rice, and we did not carry it. I told him so politely. He did not take it politely. He wanted to know why, and then he wanted to know who was in charge, and his voice grew louder with each question until two other shoppers turned to stare.

Ms. Petrakis appeared at the end of the aisle like a ship coming into harbor, calm and steady. She listened, nodded, and offered to order the rice for him. By the time he left, he was almost smiling.

Later, in the break room, she wrote in the daily log that we had "a spirited conversation with a customer in aisle nine." I read it over her shoulder and laughed out loud.

"That man was yelling," I said.

"He was passionate," she said. "Passionate customers come back. Angry ones don't. I'd rather write down the version that might come true."

I thought about that for the rest of my shift. Words, it turned out, were tools too, as useful as the box cutter, and just as easy to use carelessly. Two weeks later, the man in the gray jacket returned for his rice. He thanked Ms. Petrakis by name, and he even nodded to me.$q$, 'draft')
