-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c639ac32-73f1-5922-9044-9b583b0e2340', 'rg9-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Fine.$q$, $q$Jun and Marisol had been best friends since fourth grade, and in all that time they had never had a real argument. So when Jun texted to ask if Marisol could switch their study session from Thursday to Friday, he did not think twice about her reply. It was one word: "Fine."

By dinner, though, the word had grown in his mind like a weed. Was it fine, as in okay? Or was it fine, as in "I am annoyed, and you should know why"? He looked at the period after the word. It seemed to stare back at him.

His older sister, Hana, glanced at his phone and said, "Ouch. That period is cold." Jun asked how punctuation could have a temperature. Hana shrugged. "A period ends a conversation. An exclamation point opens it. You know that."

Jun did know that, somehow. If Marisol had written "Fine!" he would have pictured her smiling. If she had written "sure," he would have pictured her shrugging. "Fine." felt like a door closing quietly but firmly.

He spent an hour drafting a reply. Each version sounded worse than the last. Finally he gave up and called her instead.

Marisol answered on the first ring, laughing about something her cat had done. When Jun brought up the text, she was clearly confused. "I was carrying groceries," she said. "I typed with one thumb. I meant fine, like fine."

After they hung up, Jun realized that the cold period had been his own invention. The same word, it turned out, could mean very different things depending on who was reading it, and when.$q$, 'draft')
