-- Passage rg9-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('29d9d21a-0815-5fb6-9f1c-6aad492f292c', 'rg9-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Four Generations on the Wing$q$, $q$Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies leave the northern United States and southern Canada and fly south, some traveling more than two thousand miles to forests in the mountains of central Mexico. For an insect that weighs less than a paper clip, the journey is an astonishing feat.

What makes the trip even stranger is that no single butterfly completes the full cycle. In spring, monarchs leave Mexico and head north, but they live only a few weeks. They lay eggs on milkweed plants and die. Their offspring continue the trip, and their offspring continue it again. Scientists often describe the northward return as a relay race, with each generation carrying the journey a little farther before passing it on.

The last generation of the summer is different. These butterflies live for many months instead of a few weeks, long enough to fly all the way south and spend the winter in Mexico. They gather on fir trees in such numbers that the branches droop under their weight. Visitors have described the sight as a forest wearing a coat of orange.

How does a butterfly that has never made the trip know where to go? Researchers believe monarchs use the position of the sun, along with a kind of internal clock, to keep their direction steady. Some evidence suggests they may also sense Earth's magnetic field.

In recent decades, monarch numbers have declined, partly because milkweed has become harder to find. Some scientists describe the population as fragile. Others use the stronger word "endangered." Either way, many communities now plant milkweed along roads and in schoolyards, hoping to keep this remarkable migration alive.$q$, 'draft')
