   $q$Scientists describe the northward return as "a relay race." What does this comparison help explain?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The butterflies compete against each other to reach Mexico first.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The butterflies fly extremely fast for short distances.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"},{"text":"A single butterfly carries a message all the way north.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Each generation travels part of the way, then the next one continues.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Each generation travels part of the way, then the next one continues.$q$, $q$In a relay, runners each cover part of the course, just as "each generation" carries the journey "a little farther before passing it on." The butterflies are not competing; they share the journey.$q$, 'draft', '29d9d21a-0815-5fb6-9f1c-6aad492f292c')
